"""Custom metric: {{METRIC_NAME}}.

{{METRIC_DESCRIPTION}}

Register it in the eval config: name it in `criteria` with its threshold, and
point `custom_metrics` at this function by its dotted path. `CONFIG_SNIPPET`
below is that entry; merge it into the config file:

{{CONFIG_SNIPPET_TEXT}}

`custom_metrics.<name>` takes `code_config.name` (the dotted path), an optional
`description`, and an optional `metric_info` whose value range lives under
`metric_value_info.interval` (`min_value` / `max_value`). Any other key is
ignored. Without `metric_info`, ADK registers the metric with the interval
0.0-1.0.

The module has to be importable from wherever the evaluation runs, because ADK
resolves the path with `importlib.import_module`. Keeping it inside the agent
package is the reliable way to arrange that.

**The one gotcha.** `_CustomMetricEvaluator` copies the `EvalMetric` and sets
`eval_metric.threshold = None` before calling this function. Reading
`eval_metric.threshold` therefore always gives `None`, and a comparison against
it raises or silently passes everything. The threshold lives on
`eval_metric.criterion.threshold`.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

